`Game.playTurn()` composes three distinct calls instead of fusing roll-and-move-and-resolve into one method.

```java
void playTurn() {
    Player current = players.get(currentPlayerIndex);
    int roll = dice.roll();
    int rawPosition = current.getPosition() + roll;
    int finalPosition = board.resolvePosition(rawPosition);
    current.setPosition(finalPosition);
    if (finalPosition == board.getFinalCell()) {
        winner = current;
    }
    advanceTurn();
}
```

Each line is independently testable: you can assert on `board.resolvePosition(raw)` without a `Game`, or on `dice.roll()` without a board at all. Fusing these into one method is the fastest way to make this untestable later.

`Board.resolvePosition(rawPosition)` is a single lookup, called every turn regardless of what's actually at that cell.

```java
int resolvePosition(int rawPosition) {
    if (rawPosition > finalCell) {
        return currentPositionBeforeThisRoll; // overshoot: no move
    }
    Jump jump = jumpMap.get(rawPosition);
    return (jump != null) ? jump.getEnd() : rawPosition;
}
```

`jumpMap` is a `Map<Integer, Jump>` keyed by each jump's start cell, built once from board config. Snakes and ladders are the same lookup — a snake's `end` is just numerically less than its `start`.
